  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and thunderstorms will diminish this\nevening, and patchy dense fog will form west of the Blue Ridge\ntonight.\n\nShowers and thunderstorm coverage is waning at this hour as \ninstability and dewpoints drop from the northwest. With the cold\nfront moving southeast into North Carolina, the severe and \nhydro risks from earlier this afternoon are vanishing quickly.\nWhile a few leftover showers could linger across the Piedmont\nthis evening, drier air should bring decreasing clouds later\ntonight. With skies clearing west of the Blue Ridge and notable\nground moisture still in place, patchy dense fog will develop\nlater tonight into Saturday morning. The fog should burn off\nduring Saturday, while the cold front stalls to the south. Any\nchance of convection on Saturday afternoon should remain\nconfined mostly to the North Carolina and Virginia border.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heat builds into next week.\n\nAs conditions turn drier due to high pressure building across\nthe Appalachian Mountains on Sunday, a sprawling upper ridge \nexpanding from the Plains towards the Southeast will focus a \nreturn to above normal temperatures with 90s prevalent in the \nPiedmont/Roanoke/Lexington areas and reach around 90 in portions\nof the New River Valley. Long-range models start to show some \ntroughiness moving into the Northeast late next week, which will \npush the ridge southwest and turn the flow towards the \nnorthwest. This pattern would open the door for potential \norganized convective storm clusters around the Thursday to \nFriday timeframe, but there is still some model disagreement as \nto how and when the ridge weakens.",
